  "final_text": "I've built the dashboard and the alert rule. The rule is enabled and its first run succeeded with no alerts.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view has 14,074 web requests, timestamped 2026-10-04 to 2026-12-03. Useful fields:\n- **Response code** (`response.keyword`): only three values appear \u2014 200 (12,832), 404 (801) and 503 (441).\n- **Size and client:** bytes transferred, client IP, URL, host, referer and file extension.\n- **Visitor details:** operating system, browser, and source/destination country with coordinates.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-a6cee469 Web logs overview\"** (id `8f687593-b3b5-40f2-9a5f-b810fcf3f076`) has 8 panels:\n- Four headline numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct client IPs), average bytes per request, and total bytes served.\n- A stacked bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A donut chart of requests by response code.\n- Two tables: the top 10 requested URLs, and the top 10 source countries with request and visitor counts.\n\nThe default time range is `now-7d` to `now+60d`, because much of the sample data is dated in the future.\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-a6cee469 5xx spike\"** (id `250ab6b3-01ed-41b4-a422-f37a2104ffef`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`:\n- It runs every minute.\n- It counts requests with a response code from 500 to 599 in the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n\nI changed one default after creating it. Out of the box, the rule skips requests it already counted in the previous run. Because the 5-minute windows overlap, that would undercount errors, so I turned it off.\n\nThe rule has no notification actions, so alerts only show up in Kibana's alerts view. Add a connector (email, Slack, etc.) if you want to be notified.",
